Overview: 

The goal of this repository is to show the implementation details of:

Basic Auth: Sending credentials in the request header.

JWT Auth: Authenticating once to receive a token, then using that token for subsequent requests.

Authentication Methods:

1. Basic Authentication
The simplest form of security. The client sends a username and password encoded in Base64 in every request.

Header: Authorization: Basic <Base64-encoded-credentials>

Best for: Internal APIs or simple testing.

Pros: Easy to implement; no token management.

Cons: Credentials sent with every request; less secure without HTTPS.



2. JWT (JSON Web Token) Authentication
A stateless authentication mechanism where the server generates a cryptographically signed token after the user logs in.

Flow:

User sends login credentials.

Server validates and returns a JWT.

User stores the token (localStorage/Cookies).

User sends the token in the Authorization: Bearer <token> header for all protected routes.

Best for: Microservices, Single Page Applications (SPAs), and mobile apps.
